  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 207 AM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nTHUNDERSTORM CHANCES:\n\nEARLY THIS MORNING...Scattered thunderstorms are expected to develop \nduring the predawn hours and persist into this morning, mainly \nalong/east of I-135, within a zone of strengthening 850-700mb \nwarm/moist advection. Adequate elevated instability amidst decent \ndirectional effective shear may support a handful of strong to \nmarginally severe thunderstorms capable of dime-quarter size hail. \nThe potential for higher-end severe hail will likely be hampered by \nhigher cloud bases limiting buoyancy and effective shear. \nAdditionally, the potential for numerous/training storms may support \nlocally heavy rainfall and minor flooding concerns. All activity \nwill likely exit to the southeast by midday.\n\nLATE TUESDAY--TUESDAY NIGHT...A weak cold front approaching from the \nnorthwest should support increasing chances for isolated to \nscattered thunderstorms late Tuesday afternoon through Tuesday \nnight. A seasonable buoyancy/shear combination along with decent \ndowndraft CAPE may support a few strong to severe thunderstorms, \nespecially along/northwest of the KS Turnpike. \n\nWEDNESDAY NIGHT...Additional shortwave energy dropping south across \nthe Upper Mississippi Valley may support low thunderstorm chances \ngetting into central KS Wednesday night, but thinking most of this \nactivity should remain west-northwest of the forecast area.\n\nFRIDAY NIGHT--SUNDAY NIGHT...The upper ridge should finally start \nshifting east from the southwest to the south-central CONUS toward \nthe weekend, as shortwave energy tracks across the northern CONUS \nand southern Canada. This should allow a cold front to slowly \napproach from the north, with low thunderstorms chances over mainly \nthe northern half of KS this weekend. \n\nTEMPERATURES:\n\nWith northwest flow aloft persisting across the region, near to \nslightly above average temperatures are expected through late week, \nwith forecast highs mostly in the upper 80s through mid-90s, warmest \nover southern Kansas. As we head toward late this week and the \nweekend, the stout upper ridge should shift east from the southwest \nCONUS to the south-central CONUS, supporting a warming trend over \nthe weekend into at least early next week. Could be looking at \nanother round of widespread 100s, although at least dewpoints should \nbe lower and south-southwest winds on the breezy side.",
